/* data/learn-c/phy-12-03.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-03.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Boyle's, Charles' and Gay-Lussac's laws combine into one equation of state for a fixed amount of gas: PV = nRT. The same law can be written with the number of molecules N and Boltzmann's constant. This chapter explains these gas laws from the motion of molecules (kinetic theory), then moves on to heat radiation from hot bodies.","f":[{"x":"P*V = n*R*T","n":"Ideal gas equation; n = moles, R = 8.314 J/(mol K).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"},{"x":"P*V = N*k_B*T","n":"Same law with N = number of molecules; k_B = Boltzmann constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"},{"x":"R = N_A*k_B","n":"N_A = Avogadro number.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.1, p.56"}]},"3.2":{"s":"A gas has a huge number of molecules (about 10²⁵ per cubic metre) moving at random, so following each one with Newton's laws is hopeless. Kinetic theory makes simple assumptions about the molecules and works with averages, then links those averages to the large-scale quantities P, V, T and energy."},"3.3":{"s":"An ideal gas has no forces between its molecules and obeys PV = nRT at all pressures and temperatures. Real gases have molecular attractions and molecules of finite size. They behave nearly ideally at low pressure and high temperature, when the molecules are far apart, as long as the gas is well above its liquefaction temperature."},"3.4":{"s":"Gas molecules move in straight lines between collisions. The mean free path is the average distance a molecule travels between two collisions. It is shorter when the gas is denser and when the molecules are bigger: it varies as 1/d², not 1/d. Lowering the pressure, as in vacuum coating of metals, makes it longer.","f":[{"x":"lambda = 1/(sqrt(2)*pi*d^2*(N/V))","n":"Mean free path; d = molecular diameter, N/V = number of molecules per unit volume.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.4, p.57"}],"w":["Doubling the molecule's diameter cuts the mean free path to a quarter."]},"3.5":{"s":"Molecules hit the walls of the container and bounce back elastically, each hit giving the wall a small push. Adding up the momentum change per second over all molecules gives the pressure. It depends on the number of molecules per unit volume, their mass and the average of the square of their speed. The result holds for any shape of container.","f":[{"x":"P = (1/3)*(N/V)*m*<v^2>","n":"Pressure of an ideal gas; <v^2> = mean of the squared speeds, m = mass of one molecule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.5, p.59"}],"w":["The average used is of v², not of v: the mean square speed."]},"3.6":{"s":"The root mean square (rms) speed is the square root of the mean of the squared molecular speeds. From the pressure formula and PV = nRT it depends only on temperature and molar mass. Lighter molecules move faster at the same temperature: at 300 K nitrogen molecules average about 517 m/s and oxygen about 483 m/s. Molecular speeds are spread out (Maxwell's distribution).","f":[{"x":"v_rms = sqrt(3*R*T/M_0)","n":"rms speed; M_0 = molar mass in kg/mol, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.6, p.60"}],"w":["Use the molar mass in kg/mol: 28 g/mol for N₂ is 0.028 kg/mol.","The rms speed ∝ √T: doubling the speed needs four times the absolute temperature."]},"3.7":{"s":"Combining PV = (2/3)E with the gas law shows that the average kinetic energy of a gas molecule is proportional to the absolute temperature. So temperature is a measure of the average random kinetic energy of the molecules; for an ideal gas, all of the internal energy is this kinetic energy.","f":[{"x":"E/N = (3/2)*k_B*T","n":"Average translational kinetic energy per molecule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.7, p.60"},{"x":"P*V = (2/3)*E","n":"E = total translational kinetic energy of the gas.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.7, p.60"}],"w":["The average KE per molecule at a given T is the same for every gas; only the speed differs with mass."]},"3.8":{"s":"Each independent way a molecule can store energy (a degree of freedom, giving one squared term in its energy) gets on average ½ kT. A monatomic molecule has 3 translational degrees of freedom. A rigid diatomic molecule adds 2 rotational ones (rotation about its own axis does not count). A vibrating molecule adds a kinetic and a potential term for each vibration mode. The law fails at very low temperatures, where quantum effects matter.","f":[{"x":"E = (1/2)*k_B*T","n":"Average energy for each quadratic term (degree of freedom) in thermal equilibrium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.8, p.62"}],"w":["Each vibration mode counts twice (kinetic + potential), so it adds kT, not ½kT."]},"3.9":{"s":"A gas has two specific heats: at constant volume and at constant pressure. At constant pressure the gas also does work as it expands, so more heat is needed: Mayer's relation says the molar values differ by R. Using equipartition, the molar specific heats, and their ratio γ, follow from the number of degrees of freedom: monatomic, rigid diatomic, vibrating diatomic and polyatomic gases all give different values.","f":[{"x":"C_p - C_v = R","n":"Mayer's relation for molar specific heats.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"S_p - S_v = R/(M_0*J)","n":"Principal (per kg) specific heats with heat in calories; J = mechanical equivalent of heat.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"C_v = (3/2)*R","n":"Monatomic gas; C_p = (5/2)R and gamma = 5/3.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.63"},{"x":"C_v = (5/2)*R","n":"Rigid diatomic gas; C_p = (7/2)R and gamma = 7/5.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.64"},{"x":"gamma = (4 + f)/(3 + f)","n":"Polyatomic gas with f vibrational modes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.9, p.64"}],"w":["Cp is always larger than Cv for a gas, so γ is always more than 1."]},"3.10":{"s":"When thermal radiation falls on an object, part of it is absorbed, part is reflected and part passes through. The fractions of the incident energy are the coefficients of absorption a, reflection r and transmission t, and they add up to 1. Glass and quartz let heat radiation through (diathermanous); wood and iron do not (athermanous). The coefficients also depend on the wavelength of the radiation.","f":[{"x":"a + r + t_r = 1","n":"The three fractions of the incident heat energy Q: a = Q_a/Q (absorbed), r = Q_r/Q (reflected), t_r = Q_t/Q (transmitted). They have no unit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.10, p.65"}],"w":["For an opaque object t = 0, so a + r = 1: a good absorber is a poor reflector.","A perfect blackbody has a = 1, so r = 0 and t = 0."]},"3.11":{"s":"A perfect blackbody absorbs all radiation falling on it (absorptivity 1) and reflects none. Lamp black comes close. Good absorbers are also good emitters, and good reflectors are poor emitters, which is why vacuum flasks are silvered. Ferry's blackbody is a double-walled hollow sphere, blackened inside, with a small hole: radiation that enters is reflected many times and absorbed, so the hole acts as a blackbody. A heated cavity gives out blackbody (cavity) radiation.","w":["It is the small hole, not the sphere, that behaves as the blackbody."]},"3.12":{"s":"Prevost's theory: every body above 0 K radiates and absorbs heat all the time. A body stays at constant temperature when it absorbs as much as it emits. The emissive power is the energy radiated per unit time per unit area. Emissivity compares a surface with a perfect blackbody at the same temperature: 1 for a blackbody, 0 for a perfect reflector, in between for real surfaces.","f":[{"x":"R = Q/(A*t)","n":"Emissive power: energy Q radiated per unit area per unit time (W/m^2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.12, p.67"},{"x":"e = R/R_B","n":"Emissivity: emissive power compared with a perfect blackbody's at the same temperature.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.12, p.68"}],"w":["A body at room temperature still radiates; it also absorbs the same amount from its surroundings."]},"3.13":{"s":"Kirchhoff's law: at a given temperature, the ratio of a body's emissive power to its absorptivity is the same for all bodies and equals the emissive power of a perfect blackbody. So a body's emissivity equals its absorptivity, wavelength by wavelength. A good absorber of some radiation is a good emitter of the same radiation.","f":[{"x":"a = e","n":"Absorptivity equals emissivity at the same temperature (and wavelength).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.13, p.68"}],"w":["This is why cooking pots are blackened at the bottom (absorb well) and polished on top."]},"3.14":{"s":"A blackbody's radiation is spread over many wavelengths. At each temperature the power rises with wavelength to a peak and then falls. As temperature rises, the total power (area under the curve) increases and the peak moves to shorter wavelengths: Wien's displacement law. Classical theories failed to explain the whole curve; Planck succeeded by assuming energy comes in quanta hν. Wien's law gives the temperature of the Sun and stars from their colour.","f":[{"x":"lambda_max*T = b","n":"Wien's displacement law; b = 2.897 × 10^-3 m K, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.14, p.70"}],"w":["λmax is the wavelength of greatest intensity, not the longest wavelength emitted."]},"3.15":{"s":"A hot body gives out heat radiation at a rate that grows very fast with its temperature. Stefan-Boltzmann law: the power radiated per unit area by a perfect blackbody is proportional to the fourth power of its absolute temperature. An ordinary body radiates a fraction e (its emissivity) of that. In surroundings at temperature T₀, the net loss depends on the difference of the fourth powers.","f":[{"x":"R = sigma*T^4","n":"Radiant power per unit area of a perfect blackbody; sigma = Stefan's constant = 5.67 × 10^-8 W m^-2 K^-4; T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"Q/(A*t) = sigma*T^4","n":"The same law as energy Q radiated in time t from area A.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"R = e*sigma*T^4","n":"An ordinary (not black) body; e = emissivity, between 0 and 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"},{"x":"R_net = e*sigma*(T^4 - T_0^4)","n":"Net power per unit area lost by a body at T in surroundings at T_0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 3.15, p.71"}],"w":["Use kelvin, and take the fourth powers first: T⁴ − T₀⁴, not (T − T₀)⁴.","Doubling the absolute temperature makes the radiated power 16 times larger."]}};
